 orient GitHub repository, pull request, and issue work through the connected GitHub app. Use when the user asks for general GitHub help, wants PR or issue summaries, or needs repository context before choosing a more sp (file: r10/github/SKILL.md)\n- github:yeet: Publish local changes to GitHub by confirming scope, committing intentionally, pushing the branch, and opening a draft PR through the GitHub app from this plugin, with `gh` used only as a fallback where connector coverage is insuf (file: r10/yeet/SKILL.md)\n- gmail:gmail: Manage Gmail inbox triage, mailbox search, thread summaries, action extraction, reply drafting, and email forwarding through connected Gmail data. Use when the user wants to inspect a mailbox or thread, search email with Gmail qu (file: r11/gmail/SKILL.md)\n- gmail:gmail-inbox-triage: Triage a Gmail inbox into actionable buckets such as urgent, needs reply soon, waiting, and FYI using connected Gmail data. Use when the user asks to triage the inbox, rank what needs attention, find what still needs a reply, or se (file: r11/gmail-inbox-triage/SKILL.md)\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when a task needs AI-created bitmap visuals such as character cards, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, photos, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when creating new images, transforming e (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Build PowerPoint PPTX decks with artifact-tool presentation JSX (file: r14/presentations/26.521.10419/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- remotion-best-practices: Best practices for Remotion - Video creation in React (file: r0/remotion/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use this skill when a user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets-targeted spreadsheet artifacts with formulas, formatting, charts, tabl (file: r14/spreadsheets/26.521.10419/skills/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Chec (file: r12/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:upgrade-stripe: Guide for upgrading Stripe API versions and SDKs (file: r12/upgrade-stripe/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-emulator-qa: Use when validating Android feature flows in an emulator with adb-driven launch, input, UI-tree inspection, screenshots, and logcat capture. (file: r13/android-emulator-qa/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-performance: Gather and interpret Android performance evidence on an adb target using Simpleperf CPU profiles, Perfetto or Compose traces, gfxinfo frame data, dumpsys meminfo snapshots, Java heap dumps, and native allocation traces. Use when  (file: r13/android-performance/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + short path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths after expanding the matching alias from `### Skill roots`.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, expand the listed short `path` with the matching alias from `### Skill roots`, then open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the directory containing that expanded `SKILL.md` first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Browser`: Browser / browser-use plugin Aliases: @browser, @browser-use, browser-use, Browser, in-app browser. Use Browser, the Codex in-app browser, when the user asks to open, inspect, navigate, test, click, type, or screenshot local web targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file:// URLs, or the current in-app browser tab. After significant frontend changes to a local app, use Browser to open the relevant local target when it is known or obvious, unless the user asks for another browser tool. For requests like \"open localhost:3000\" or \"open to localhost:4000\", navigate the in-app browser to http://localhost:3000 or http://localhost:4000. Do not satisfy explicit @browser or @browser-use requests with macOS `open`, shell commands, or generic web browsing unless the user asks for another browser tool or approves a fallback.\n- `Build iOS Apps`: Build iOS apps with workflows for App Intents, SwiftUI UI work, refactors, performance profiling, leak investigation, and simulator debugging.\n- `Build macOS Apps`: Build, run, test, debug, instrument, and implement local macOS apps using Xcode, SwiftUI, AppKit interop, unified logging, and shell-first desktop workflows.\n- `Build Web Apps`: Build web apps with frontend asset design, browser testing, UI components, payments, and database guidance.\n- `Chrome`: Chrome automation for remote URLs, authenticated/profile-dependent pages, existing Chrome tabs, cookies, extensions, and Codex Chrome Extension setup.\n- `CircleCI`: Build, test, and deploy any application\n- `Cloudflare`: Cloudflare platform plugin with curated skills for Workers, Wrangler, and Agents SDK plus the official Cloudflare API MCP server.\n- `CodeRabbit`: AI-powered code review in Codex, powered by CodeRabbit.\n- `Computer Use`: Control desktop apps on macOS from Codex through Computer Use.\n- `Documents`: Create and edit document artifacts in Codex, including Word files and Google Docs.\n- `Game Studio`: Design, prototype, and ship browser games with guided 2D and 3D workflows, asset pipelines, and playtesting support.\n- `GitHub`: Inspect repositories, triage pull requests and issues, debug CI, and publish changes through a hybrid GitHub connector and CLI workflow.\n- `Gmail`: Work with Gmail using the configured Gmail app connector.\n- `Presentations`: Create, edit, render, verify, and export presentation slide decks. Use when Codex needs to build or modify a deck, slidedeck, presentation deck, slide deck, slides, PowerPoint, Google Slides, PPT, PPTX, .ppt, or .pptx file.\n- `Spreadsheets`: Create, edit, analyze, visualize, render, and export spreadsheets or Google Sheets-ready workbooks in Codex.\n- `Stripe`: Payments and business tools\n- `Test Android Apps`: Test Android apps with emulator workflows for reproduction, screenshots, UI inspection, log capture, and performance profiling.\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -> specific):\n\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/<skill-name>/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally <= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `<file>:<line_start>-<line_end>|note=[<how memory was used>]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `<rollout_ids>` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `<timestamp>-<short slug>.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\n## User Profile\n\nThe user works across several local game and tooling projects at once and uses Codex, Chrome, Finder, Telegram, Godot, and desktop AI-chat windows as one working surface. They often work in Chinese, but they preserve exact English paths, commands, UI labels, ports, filenames, and error strings when those are the real routing handles. [chronicle memory]\n\nRecent high-activity scopes are `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/GameGmeta`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/cc0-game-assets-library`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame-dock`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame`, and `/Users/vincet/Documents/skills/codex-memory-backup`. The newest Chronicle lane on May 26 is a browser-first `GameGmeta` map-editor/export loop tied tightly to the local CC0 vault: the user was testing grass-base layering, pack switching, Godot JSON export, browser-to-Godot transfer, and Tiny-style asset-library expansion in parallel. [chronicle memory]\n\nFor game work, they repeatedly validate against the live runtime surface instead of trusting a code summary. “Done” usually means a real Godot debug window, a browser-visible local export, a visible owned-library state, or a simulator/browser confirmation, not only changed files. [chronicle memory]\n\nThey use local asset catalogs as production tools, not just references. The `LOCAL 2D ASSET VAULT` is an active source-of-truth for downloaded packs, extracted `Preview.png` checks, favorites/shortlists, license context, and adjacent prototypes such as `GameGmeta`. [chronicle memory]\n\nTheir repeated steering pattern is practical and defect-oriented: if a prototype map is “unreasonable,” an editor/export handoff is flaky, a payment state is unclear, or an automation did not really send, they want the concrete failing surface traced and fixed rather than abstract advice. [chronicle memory]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Use the live surface as truth: Godot debug windows, Chrome pages, iPhone Simulator, Finder artifact views, and Codex automation panels outrank an unverified code-only claim. [chronicle memory]\n- Preserve exact routing handles first: project paths, filenames, commands, ports, error strings, bundle ids, config keys, automation names, thread titles, model names, and visible UI copy. [chronicle memory]\n- In Godot work, move quickly from plan to a runnable debug window or browser export; do not stop at ideation if the next step can be a playable prototype. [chronicle memory]\n- When a browser or Godot prototype map looks “unreasonable,” treat that as a structural defect: rebuild from the reference map or complete asset set instead of polishing the old placeholder layout. [chronicle memory]\n- For `GameGmeta`, start from complete local map assets instead of a generic tiled placeholder map, then layer POIs/HUD/wallet/avatar behavior on top. [chronicle memory]\n- For `GameGmeta` map flows, verify the real browser path: `map cards -> enter map -> Maps button -> return -> enter another map`. [chronicle memory]\n- In `GameGmeta 地图编辑器`, preserve the rule that assets are layered on top of green/grass background rather than replacing it; keep grass as base terrain and treat placed materials as upper-layer objects. [chronicle memory]\n- When the browser editor looks correct, do not stop at `导出 Godot JSON`; carry the handoff through `editor_godot_map.json` replacement, Godot checks, and a refreshed browser/runtime page. [chronicle memory]\n- When the visible player sprite is not readable enough, use the local asset library to pick a better multi-direction animation sheet instead of defending a tiny placeholder sprite. [chronicle memory]\n- When spinning out a new asset workspace like `metaverse-ui-assets`, preserve full package structure with `License.txt`, `Preview.png`, and tilesheet/tilemap context rather than copying loose images. [chronicle memory]\n- When bootstrapping a new repo from a curated asset set, prefer a lightweight manifest/README/doc skeleton before importing thousands of assets into the repo. [chronicle memory]\n- Use the local `LOCAL 2D ASSET VAULT` as the source-of-truth for downloaded CC0 assets; direct Chrome preview of extracted `Preview.png` files is valid evidence. [chronicle memory]\n- When the next asset hunt is Tiny-town/cozy-village oriented, normalize the vault wording under `Tiny 风格` before searching again so older naming does not pollute the shortlist. [chronicle memory]\n- When triaging candidate downloaded packs, use the vault’s `收藏` / `已收藏` flow as the working shortlist instead of a loose external bookmark trail. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, keep early versions small when the request is “a dungeon treasure-hunting game” and “not add dungeon enemies,” then grow systems only after the loop is playable. [chronicle memory]\n- Once a playable `godot-2d-trea…3337 tokens truncated…s Godot; browser/login/payment/share/analytics pushes the choice toward a web stack. [chronicle memory]\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker\n\n- Chapter compression, Tilesetter Lite, map-edge refinement, wall-material fixes, and non-spoiler HUD guidance: `Tilesetter Lite.app`, `selected-8-chapters-story-menu.png`, `oriented_wall_tile`, `Next: Find the Stone Key`\n  - desc: Covers `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker`: 8-chapter story/menu integration, local `godot4` setup, map-edge refinement, import-cache traps, vertical-wall follow-up, and HUD/objective guidance without puzzle spoilers. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure\n\n- Demo publishing, hosting-page metadata, and backend-mediated full unlock planning: `Quiet Dungeon Treasure Demo`, `payment was not configured`, `order_id`, `checkout_url`, `NOWPayments`, `Coinbase Commerce`\n  - desc: Covers the May 22 distribution/monetization track in `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`: Steam blockage, hosting-page pivot, demo/full split, repo hygiene, and backend/webhook unlock planning. [chronicle memory]\n\n- AI challenge state bridge, chapter-menu polish, and late-stage combat/follower validation: `quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_state.json`, `quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_command.json`, `Choose Chapter`, `Challenge`, `AI Challenge`\n  - desc: Covers the still-supported `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure` live-debug workflow around JSON-driven AI Challenge control, chapter-card/menu polish, teammate spacing/pathing, ranged-projectile cleanup, and repeated Chapter 1-4 runtime verification. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### workflow:interactive-narrative-market-research\n\n- `影游互动类游戏` research and `Normalville Alien Dinner Theater` branch framework: `影游互动类游戏`, `Normalville Alien Dinner Theater`, `imagegen`, `每小时生成外星人剧场分支`\n  - desc: Covers the concept workflow that moved from market framing into a concrete alien-dinner-theater structure, suspect rules, lineup generation, and hourly branch/still generation. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### workflow:ai-game-platform-ui-automation\n\n- Lightweight wallet-login game platform UI planning and recurring design automation: `AI游戏平台UI功能设计优化`, `AIGame Dock`, `Play Demo`, `Buy Full Game`, `Download`, `My Games`\n  - desc: Covers the demo-first game-platform UI framing, the recurring same-thread automation, and the later mockup simplification toward discovery/demo/pay/download states. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### workflow:game-ai-industry-automation\n\n- Steam-upload planning plus `hourly-game-ai-industry-scan` email-delivery fallback: `如何在steam上上传游戏`, `demo/full-version unlock`, `hourly-game-ai-industry-scan`, `游戏AI行业简报自动化测试`, `automatic permission approval review did not finish before its deadline`, `Message sent`\n  - desc: Covers the May 24 Chinese AI-chat workflow that mixed publishing/payment-state planning with a game-AI email automation test, including the demo/full unlock checklist and the Gmail permission-timeout versus manual-send split. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### workflow:codex-desktop-session-history\n\n- Codex folder thread visibility and global local-storage explanation: `本文件夹其他线程记录为什么不显示`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/sessions`, `session_index.jsonl`, `logs_2.sqlite`, `state_5.sqlite`\n  - desc: Explains why other thread records did not show for the current folder and which local `.codex` files hold the truth on this machine. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/skills\n\n- Hourly AI video-editing scan, Chinese Gmail brief, and dedupe memory: `hourly-ai-video-editing-tools-scan`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/automations/hourly-ai-video-editing-tools-scan/memory.md`, `每小时视频剪辑AI新工具简报`\n  - desc: Covers the recurring hourly automation that reads the dedupe baseline, finds newly surfaced AI video-editing tools, sends a concise Chinese Gmail brief, and should persist the new run back into automation memory. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/skills/codex-memory-backup\n\n- Codex account backup sync, strict split-size cap, and local-versus-remote push truth: `split -b 89000000`, `SHA256SUMS`, `main...origin/main [ahead 1]`, `FINAL_VERIFY_OK`\n  - desc: Search here for exact backup include/exclude rules, verify-before-delete rotation, strict decimal part-size handling, and the distinction between local verification and confirmed push success. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame\n\n- FlashPlay DeepSeek provider wiring and simulator validation: `com.flashplay.demo`, `providerOrder: deepseek`, `backend/.env`, `deepseek.configured: true`\n  - desc: Covers `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame` work on local DeepSeek-backed generation, backend/provider wiring, and simulator-visible validation after relaunch. [chronicle memory]\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/Users/vincet/Documents/a